<SiteLayout title="How the Solar System works" description="Understand gravity, electromagnetism, the nuclear forces, orbits, seasons, tides, eclipses and the physics of planetary systems.">
  <div class="container breadcrumbs"><a href="/">Home</a><span aria-hidden="true">/</span><span>The science</span></div>
  <header class="page-intro container"><div class="eyebrow">SOLAR SYSTEM PHYSICS</div><h1>The physics of the Solar System.</h1><p class="lede">Gravity, motion, heat and light shape the planets and their moons. This section introduces the main ideas and shows how they apply across the Solar System.</p></header>
  <section class="section container">
    <div class="section-head"><div><div class="eyebrow">The four fundamental interactions</div><h2>The four fundamental forces.</h2></div><p>Gravity and electromagnetism dominate most large-scale Solar System behaviour. The strong and weak interactions operate at the nuclear scale, including inside the Sun.</p></div>
    <div class="force-grid">{laws.map((law)=><article class="card force-card"><span class="force-number">{law.n}</span><span class="eyebrow">{law.tag}</span><h3>{law.title}</h3><p>{law.text}</p></article>)}</div>
    <div class="prose science-prose">
      <h2>1. Why do planets orbit the Sun?</h2>
      <p>A planet has forward motion, and the Sun's gravity continually accelerates it inward. The combination produces an orbit rather than a straight-line path or a direct fall into the Sun. In a simple two-body model, a gravitationally bound orbit is a conic section; planets follow ellipses with the Sun at one focus.</p>
      <p>Kepler's laws describe the geometry and timing of planetary orbits. The line from a planet to the Sun sweeps out equal areas in equal times, meaning the planet moves faster near perihelion and slower near aphelion. The square of orbital period is proportional to the cube of the orbit's semi-major axis when the central mass dominates.</p>
      <div class="equation-card"><span class="eyebrow">Kepler's third law · simplified</span><strong>T² ∝ a³</strong><p>For objects orbiting the same central mass, the orbital period squared scales with the semi-major axis cubed. Farther worlds generally take much longer to complete an orbit.</p></div>
      <h2>2. Why do we have seasons?</h2>
      <p>Seasons come primarily from a planet's axial tilt relative to its orbital plane. When a hemisphere tilts toward its star, it receives sunlight at a higher angle and usually has longer daylight hours. When it tilts away, sunlight arrives at a lower angle and days are shorter. Earth's changing distance from the Sun is not the cause of its seasons.</p>
      <h2>3. Rotation, day and night</h2>
      <p>A sidereal rotation period measures how long a world takes to turn once relative to distant stars. A solar day measures the interval from one local noon to the next. Because an orbiting planet moves while it rotates, the solar day is not always the same as the sidereal rotation period. Retrograde rotation, as on Venus, can make the relationship especially unintuitive.</p>
      <h2>4. Tides: gravity across a body</h2>
      <p>Tides are caused by differences in gravitational acceleration across an extended body. The near side and far side of an ocean, moon or planet do not experience exactly the same gravitational pull. On Earth, the Moon is the dominant tidal influence, with the Sun also contributing. Tidal forces can heat moons internally and alter orbital energy over long timescales.</p>
      <h2>5. Eclipses and alignments</h2>
      <p>A solar eclipse occurs when the Moon passes between Earth and the Sun and its shadow reaches part of Earth's surface. A lunar eclipse occurs when Earth passes between the Sun and Moon and Earth's shadow falls on the Moon. They do not happen every month because the Moon's orbital plane is tilted by about five degrees relative to Earth's orbit.</p>
      <h2>6. Why do sunsets look red?</h2>
      <p>Sunlight contains many wavelengths. When the Sun is low, its light travels through a longer path in the atmosphere. Shorter blue wavelengths are scattered more strongly out of the direct beam by molecules in air — a process called Rayleigh scattering. The remaining direct sunlight is enriched in longer wavelengths, which can make the Sun and nearby sky look orange or red. Aerosols and clouds can alter the colours too.</p>
      <h2>7. Why does the greenhouse effect matter?</h2>
      <p>A planet's surface absorbs energy and emits infrared radiation. Greenhouse gases absorb and re-emit some of that infrared radiation, changing how efficiently energy escapes to space. The result depends on atmospheric composition, pressure, clouds, circulation, surface properties and incoming sunlight. Venus demonstrates how a very thick carbon-dioxide atmosphere can produce an extreme surface greenhouse environment.</p>
    </div>
